    function GroupBy(const AColumn: String): TSplom; overload;
    /// <summary>Splits every numeric panel by the groups of string column <c>column</c> (GGally's <c>ggpairs(aes(colour = group))</c>); an empty palette uses the default level palette.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>Calls the C++ method <c>datamunge::Splom::group_by</c>.</para>
    /// <para>The column need not be a panel variable but must be a string column of the source frame. Groups are its sorted distinct non-missing values, and group k takes <c>palette[k % palette.size()]</c>. Each group gets its own points, diagonal density, smooth and prediction band, and density contours; rows with a missing group are left out of those panels. When the column is also a panel variable its rainclouds, bars, and mosaic axis share the colors. Pass "" to clear the grouping. Clears point_colors().</para>
    /// <para><b>Example</b></para>
    /// <code>
    /// auto pairs = dm::Splom(dm::datasets::iris()).group_by("Species");
    /// std::print("{}\n", pairs.groups());  // ["setosa", "versicolor", "virginica"]
    /// </code>
    /// <para>See also: <c>groups()</c>, <c>set_col()</c>.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="AColumn">String column to group by, or "" to clear.</param>
    /// <param name="APalette">Group colors in sorted-group order; empty uses the default.</param>
    /// <returns>This matrix, for chaining.</returns>
    /// <exception cref="ENativeError">Kind <c>InvalidArgument</c> (C++ <c>std::invalid_argument</c>): If <c>column</c> is not a string column of the source frame.</exception>
