    /// <para><b>Example</b></para>
    /// <code>
    /// dm::fda::HierarchicalBSpline h({0.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 1.0});
    /// std::print("{}\n", h.evaluate_grid({{0.0, 1.0}, {0.5}}).size()); // 2
    /// </code>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="AAxes">d non-empty coordinate lists.</param>
    /// <returns>Row-major values (last axis fastest).</returns>
    /// <exception cref="ENativeError">Kind <c>InvalidArgument</c> (C++ <c>std::invalid_argument</c>): On a dimension mismatch, empty axis, or outside point.</exception>
    function EvaluateGrid(const AAxes: TArrayOfArrayOfDouble): TArrayOfDouble; overload;
  end;

  /// <summary>Truncated hierarchical B-spline (THB-spline) of Giannelli, Juttler and Speleers.</summary>
  /// <remarks>
  /// <para>Wraps the C++ class <c>datamunge::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline</c>.</para>
  /// <para>Level 0 is the caller's TensorBSpline; level l + 1 is the dyadic refinement of level l. A dyadic cell tree defines the nested domains Omega^0 (whole domain) contains Omega^1 contains ..., where Omega^l is the union of cells of depth &gt;= l. A level-l basis function is active when its support lies in Omega^l but not in Omega^(l+1). The THB basis consists of the active functions, each truncated: its representation on finer levels drops the finer functions whose support lies in the finer refined domain. The resulting basis is a partition of unity, preserves the continuity of the underlying splines, and spans the hierarchical spline space.</para>
  /// <para>Internally each level stores a full coefficient net whose entries outside the next level's refined domain always equal the refinement of the coarser level; coefficient edits are pushed down through all finer levels with truncation, so evaluation needs only the finest level covering a point. Refinement never changes the represented function. Bases must be clamped. Cells are integer ids (root 0).</para>
  /// <para><b>Example</b></para>
  /// <code>
  /// auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(2, 4);
  /// dm::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline thb(dm::fda::TensorBSpline({b, b}, std::vector&lt;double&gt;(16, 1.0)));
  /// thb.refine_region({0.0, 0.0}, {0.5, 0.5}, 1);
  /// std::print("{} {:.3f}\n", thb.level_count(), thb.evaluate({0.2, 0.2})); // 2 1.000
  /// </code>
  /// <para><b>References</b></para>
  /// <list type="bullet">
  /// <item><description>C. Giannelli, B. Juttler and H. Speleers (2012). THB-splines: The truncated basis for hierarchical splines. Computer Aided Geometric Design 29(7), 485-498. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cagd.2012.03.025</description></item>
  /// <item><description>D. R. Forsey and R. H. Bartels (1988). Hierarchical B-spline refinement. https://doi.org/10.1145/378456.378512</description></item>
  /// </list>
  /// <para>See also: <c>HierarchicalBSpline</c>, <c>dyadic_refine()</c>.</para>
  /// </remarks>
  TTruncatedHierarchicalBSpline = class(TNativeObject)
  public
    /// <summary>Starts a hierarchy whose level 0 (and root cell / domain) is <c>base</c>.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>Calls the C++ constructor of <c>datamunge::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline</c>.</para>
    /// <para><b>Example</b></para>
    /// <para>See the class example.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="ABase">Level-0 spline; every axis basis must be clamped.</param>
    /// <exception cref="ENativeError">Kind <c>InvalidArgument</c> (C++ <c>std::invalid_argument</c>): If an axis basis is not clamped or d &gt; 20.</exception>
    constructor Create(ABase: TTensorBSpline); overload;
