/// <summary>Exact expected Metropolis acceptance of one HMC proposal (fixed step size and number of leapfrog steps, unit mass) on a one-dimensional</summary>
/// <remarks>
/// <para>Calls C++ <c>datamunge::bayes::leapfrog_gaussian_acceptance</c>.</para>
/// <para><c>N(m,s^2)</c> target, started in equilibrium.</para>
/// <para>In scaled coordinates <c>x=((q-m)/s,\,p)</c> each leapfrog step is the linear map <c>M=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\-h/2&amp;1\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;h\\0&amp;1\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\-h/2&amp;1\end{pmatrix}</c> with <c>h=\varepsilon/s</c>, <c>\det M=1</c> and <c>\operatorname{tr}M=2-h^2</c>, so it is stable exactly when <c>\varepsilon&lt;2s</c>. Writing <c>x_0=r(\cos\phi,\sin\phi)</c> the energy error is <c>\Delta H=r^2f(\phi)</c> with <c>f=\tfrac12(|M^L(\cos\phi,\sin\phi)|^2-1)</c>, and because <c>r^2/2\sim\text{Exp}(1)</c> the radial average of <c>\min(1,e^{-\Delta H})</c> is <c>1/(1+2f)</c> for <c>f&gt;0</c> and 1 otherwise. The angle is integrated numerically.</para>
/// <para><c>\varepsilon&gt;0</c>.</para>
/// <para><c>L\geq1</c>.</para>
/// <para><c>s&gt;0</c>.</para>
/// <para>Leaves out the C++ default arguments <c>AAngles = 4096</c> (other overloads pass them).</para>
/// </remarks>
/// <param name="AStepSize">Leapfrog step size</param>
/// <param name="ANumSteps">Number of leapfrog steps</param>
/// <param name="ASd">Target standard deviation</param>
/// <returns>The expected acceptance probability.</returns>
/// <exception cref="ENativeError">Kind <c>InvalidArgument</c> (C++ <c>std::invalid_argument</c>): for non-positive arguments.</exception>
function LeapfrogGaussianAcceptance(AStepSize: Double; ANumSteps: Int64; ASd: Double): Double; overload;
/// <summary>Exact expected Metropolis acceptance of one HMC proposal (fixed step size and number of leapfrog steps, unit mass) on a one-dimensional</summary>
